Mean integrated Brier score over (0.25, 6): Cox 0.1521, Kaplan–Meier 0.1946, explained 21.8%.
\image(figures/survival_accuracy.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Mean over 300 replications of the Brier score of a fitted Cox model at horizons from 0.25 to 6 on censored test samples of 400. The thick grey curve uses the true event times, the blue curve is the IPCW estimate from the censored data, and the orange curve drops subjects censored before the horizon.)
\alttext(The grey oracle curve rises from about 0.03 to a peak near 0.19 around t = 3 and falls to about 0.15 at t = 6. The blue IPCW curve lies on top of it throughout. The orange complete-case curve is slightly above it up to t = 3, then falls increasingly below, reaching about 0.10 at t = 6.)

The IPCW estimates track the oracle at every horizon, for both the Brier score and the AUC, to within about 0.002. The complete-case shortcut is close only at early horizons, where little censoring has happened.
- Brier score. The complete-case shortcut drifts below the oracle and underestimates the error by 30% at \(t=6\). The subjects it drops are long survivors whose status at \(t\) is uncertain, so the retained sample is dominated by early events and clear survivors, which are easy to predict.
- AUC. The shortcut is optimistic in the same way, by about 0.02 at \(t=6\).

The cost of IPCW appears in the error SD column. As the censoring survival \(G(t)\) falls from 0.875 to 0.25, the weights grow and the replication-to-replication error of the IPCW Brier score quadruples. The error of the AUC triples. Beyond the last time with \(\hat G(t)>0\), no reweighting can recover the information, so the functions reject such horizons. In practice the evaluation horizon should stop well before that point.

The integrated Brier score over (0.25, 6) is 0.152 for the Cox model against 0.195 for the covariate-free Kaplan–Meier prediction, so the model explains about 22% of the prediction error. The Kaplan–Meier benchmark makes the IBS interpretable. On its own, a Brier score depends heavily on how many events occur by \(t\).

The AUC matches `riskRegression::Score` and the Brier and integrated Brier scores (with the Kaplan–Meier reference) match `pec::pec` and `pec::crps` on the `pbc` data, including tied markers and a grid that does not start at 0.

